@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+`applyPatch` validates the shape of a patch but not the `value` members it carries. Values that are not JSON (`undefined`, `NaN`, `Infinity`, functions, `bigint`, symbols, `Date`/`Map`-like objects) are accepted and silently copied or coerced into the result, so callers get documents that cannot be serialised as JSON. RFC 6902 values are JSON values (RFC 8259).
+
+## What Changes
+
+- `add`, `replace` and `test` reject a `value` that is not a JSON value, with `JsonPatchError`.
+- Not breaking for valid input: only patches that carry non-JSON values change behaviour, and those were already outside the standard. Calling this out for importers that may have relied on it.
+- Audit result: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`, pointer escaping, hostile keys and atomicity already conform to RFC 6902 §4 and §5; no change.
+
+**Governing standards:** RFC 6902 §3 and §4.1, §4.3, §4.6 (value members); RFC 8259 §3 (JSON values).
+
+**Out of scope:** validating the `document` argument, other operations, and any change to error types.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+<!-- None -->
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: the "Patch document structure" requirement gains a rule on `value` being a JSON value.
+
+## Impact
+
+- `src/index.ts`: `requireValue` validates the value.
+- `test/patch.test.ts`: new tests.
+- No new dependencies; no `package.json` change.
